"To find the 100th term of the arithmetic sequence 6, 10, 14, 18, ..., we need to use the formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:\n\n\\[ a_n = a + (n-1)d \\]\n\nwhere \\(a\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the term number.\n\nGiven:\n- The first term \\(a = 6\\),\n- The common difference \\(d = 10 - 6 = 4\\).\n\nWe need to find the 100th term (\\(n = 100\\)):\n\n\\[ a_{100} = 6 + (100-1) \\cdot 4 \\]\n\\[ a_{100} = 6 + 99 \\cdot 4 \\]\n\\[ a_{100} = 6 + 396 \\]\n\\[ a_{100} = 402 \\]\n\nTherefore, the 100th term of the sequence is \\( \\boxed{402} \\)."
