"To evaluate \\(\\log_28\\sqrt{2}\\), we start by expressing the base and the argument of the logarithm in terms of powers of 2.\n\nFirst, note that:\n\\[ 28 = 2^3 \\]\n\\[ \\sqrt{2} = 2^{1/2} \\]\n\nSo, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\\[ \\log_28\\sqrt{2} = \\log_{2^3} 2^{1/2} \\]\n\nUsing the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that \\(\\log_b a = \\frac{\\log a}{\\log b}\\), we get:\n\\[ \\log_{2^3} 2^{1/2} = \\frac{\\log (2^{1/2})}{\\log (2^3)} \\]\n\nSimplifying the expression:\n\\[ \\frac{\\log (2^{1/2})}{\\log (2^3)} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\log 2}{3\\log 2} = \\frac{1}{6} \\]\n\nTherefore, the value is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}} \\]"
