"To find the value of \\( h(6) \\) for the function \\( h(x) = \\sqrt{\\frac{x^3 + 72}{2}} + 1 \\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute \\( x = 6 \\) into the function.\n2. Simplify the expression inside the square root.\n3. Take the square root.\n4. Add 1 to the result.\n\nLet's start with the substitution:\n\n\\[ h(6) = \\sqrt{\\frac{6^3 + 72}{2}} + 1 \\]\n\nNext, simplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[ 6^3 = 216 \\]\n\\[ 216 + 72 = 288 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{288}{2} = 144 \\]\n\nNow, take the square root:\n\n\\[ \\sqrt{144} = 12 \\]\n\nFinally, add 1 to 12:\n\n\\[ 13 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( h(6) \\) is \\(\\boxed{13}\\)."
"First, we need to convert the height of the tree from feet to inches because the growth rate is given in inches.\n\nThe tree is 12 feet tall initially:\n\\[ 12 \\text{ feet} \\times 12 \\text{ inches/foot} = 144 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nThe tree needs to be 36 feet tall:\n\\[ 36 \\text{ feet} \\times 12 \\text{ inches/foot} = 432 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the total growth needed:\n\\[ 432 \\text{ inches} - 144 \\text{ inches} = 288 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nThe tree grows 18 inches per year. To find out how many years it will take to grow 288 inches, we divide the total growth needed by the growth rate:\n\\[ \\frac{288 \\text{ inches}}{18 \\text{ inches/year}} = 16 \\text{ years} \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $16$ years. I hope it is correct."
"Given the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + bx + c = 0\\), we know that the difference of the roots is \\(|b - 2c|\\). Let the roots of the equation be \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\). According to Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots \\(\\alpha + \\beta\\) is equal to \\(-b\\), and the product of the roots \\(\\alpha \\beta\\) is equal to \\(c\\).\n\nThe difference of the roots is given by:\n\\[|\\alpha - \\beta| = \\sqrt{(\\alpha + \\beta)^2 - 4\\alpha\\beta} = \\sqrt{(-b)^2 - 4c} = \\sqrt{b^2 - 4c} = |b - 2c|\\]\n\nSince \\(c \\neq 0\\), we can equate the expressions inside the square root:\n\\[b^2 - 4c = (b - 2c)^2\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying:\n\\[b^2 - 4c = b^2 - 4bc + 4c^2\\]\n\\[0 = -4bc\\]\n\\[c = \\boxed{\\frac{b}{4}}\\]"
"To find the constant coefficient of the polynomial \\(3(x - 4) + 2(x^2 - x + 7) - 5(x - 1)\\), we first need to simplify the expression.\n\nFirst, distribute the constants inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n3(x - 4) = 3x - 12\n\\]\n\\[\n2(x^2 - x + 7) = 2x^2 - 2x + 14\n\\]\n\\[\n-5(x - 1) = -5x + 5\n\\]\n\nNext, combine all the terms:\n\\[\n3x - 12 + 2x^2 - 2x + 14 - 5x + 5\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\n3x - 2x - 5x + (-12 + 14 + 5) + 2x^2\n\\]\n\\[\n-4x + 7 + 2x^2\n\\]\n\nThe constant term is the term with no \\(x\\) coefficient, which is \\(7\\).\n\nTherefore, the constant coefficient is \\(\\boxed{7}\\)."
"Let the two positive numbers be \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). We are given two conditions:\n\n1. \\( xy = 24 \\)\n2. \\( x^2 + y^2 = 73 \\)\n\nWe need to find the square of their difference, which is \\( (x - y)^2 \\).\n\nFirst, we use the identity for the square of a difference:\n\\[\n(x - y)^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 2xy\n\\]\n\nWe already know \\( x^2 + y^2 = 73 \\) and \\( xy = 24 \\). Substituting these values into the identity, we get:\n\\[\n(x - y)^2 = 73 - 2 \\cdot 24\n\\]\n\nCalculating the right-hand side:\n\\[\n(x - y)^2 = 73 - 48 = 25\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the square of their difference is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{25}\n\\]"
