"To determine the number of ways Mr.  Mr.  Brennan can pick 3 boys and 2 girls from his class, we need to calculate the number of combinations for the boys and girls separately and then multiply them together.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of ways to choose 3 boys out of 7:\n\\[\n\\binom{7}{3} = \\frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!} = \\frac{7 \\times 6 \\times 5}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 35\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 4:\n\\[\n\\binom{4}{2} = \\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!} = \\frac{4 \\times 3}{2 \\times 1} = 6\n\\]\n\nNow, we multiply these two results together to find the total number of ways to choose 3 boys and 2 girls:\n\\[\n35 \\times 6 = 210\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to pick 3 boys and 2 girls is \\boxed{210}."
