"We can use the AM-GM inequality to find the minimum value of the given expression. The AM-GM inequality states that for any non-negative real numbers $a$ and $b$, we have\n\n$$\\frac{a + b}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{ab}.$$\n\nApplying this inequality to the given expression, we get\n\n$$\\frac{x + \\frac{108}{(x - y)^3 (y - z)^2 z}}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{x \\cdot \\frac{108}{(x - y)^3 (y - z)^2 z}}.$$\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\\frac{x + \\frac{108}{(x - y)^3 (y - z)^2 z}}{2} \\ge \\sqrt{108} = 12.$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by 2, we get\n\n$$x + \\frac{108}{(x - y)^3 (y - z)^2 z} \\ge 24.$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of the given expression is $\\boxed{24}$.\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $\\boxed{24}$."
"To determine the smallest value of \\( c \\) for which the function \\( f(x) = -3x^2 + 36x - 7 \\) becomes invertible when its domain is restricted to \\( x \\in [c, \\infty) \\), we need to find the vertex of the parabola represented by the function. The vertex of a parabola given by \\( ax^2 + bx + c \\) occurs at \\( x = -\\frac{b}{2a} \\).\n\nHere, \\( a = -3 \\) and \\( b = 36 \\). Plugging these values into the vertex formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{36}{2(-3)} = -\\frac{36}{-6} = 6\n\\]\n\nThe vertex of the parabola is at \\( x = 6 \\). For the function to be invertible when restricted to \\( x \\in [c, \\infty) \\), \\( c \\) must be at least 6. This ensures that the parabola is to the right of the vertex, making the function one-to-one.\n\nThus, the smallest such value of \\( c \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]"
