"We can use the AM-GM inequality to find the minimum value of $a^2 + b^3 + c^4$. The AM-GM inequality states that for any non-negative real numbers $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$,\n\n$$\\frac{x_1 + x_2 + \\cdots + x_n}{n} \\geq \\sqrt[n]{x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n}.$$\n\nApplying this inequality to $a^2, b^3,$ c^4, we get\n\n$$\\frac{a^2 + b^3 + c^4}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{a^2 b^3 c^4}.$$\n\nSince $a, b, c$ are positive real numbers, we have\n\n$$\\sqrt[3]{a^2 b^3 c^4} = abc.$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\frac{a^2 + b^3 + c^4}{3} \\geq abc.$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by 3, we get\n\n$$a^2 + b^3 + c^4 \\geq 3abc.$$\n\nThe answer is $\\boxed{3abc}$."
