"Let the measure of each of the two congruent acute angles be \\( x \\) degrees. Therefore, the measure of each of the other three angles is \\( x + x = 2x \\) degrees.\n\nThe sum of the interior angles in any polygon is \\( 540 \\) degrees. For a pentagon, the sum of the interior angles is:\n\\[ 540 \\text{ degrees} \\]\n\nThe sum of the measures of the five angles can be expressed as:\n\\[ 2x + 2x + 2x + x + x = 6x \\]\n\nSetting this equal to the sum of the interior angles:\n\\[ 6x = 540 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{540}{6} = 90 \\]\n\nThus, the measure of each of the two congruent acute angles is \\( 90 \\) degrees. The measure of each of the other three angles is:\n\\[ 2x = 2 \\times 90 = 180 \\]\n\nTherefore, the common measure of the large angles is:\n\\[ \\boxed{180} \\]"
"To determine the weight of walnuts in the mixture, we start by understanding the given ratio of pecans, walnuts, and cashews, which is \\(2:3:1\\). This means that for every 2 parts of pecans, there are 3 parts of walnuts and 1 part of cashews.\n\nFirst, we find the total number of parts in the ratio:\n\\[\n2 + 3 + 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we know that the total weight of the mixture is 9 pounds. To find the weight of one part, we divide the total weight by the total number of parts:\n\\[\n\\frac{9 \\text{ pounds}}{6 \\text{ parts}} = 1.5 \\text{ pounds per part}\n\\]\n\nSince the walnuts make up 3 parts of the mixture, we multiply the weight of one part by 3 to find the weight of the walnuts:\n\\[\n3 \\text{ parts} \\times 1.5 \\text{ pounds part} = 4.5 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the weight of the walnuts in the mixture is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4.5}\n\\]"
"Let \\( n \\) be the number of tests Brian has taken before his last test. The arithmetic mean of his test scores before the last test is 91, so the total sum of his test scores before the last test is \\( 91n \\).\n\nIf Brian scores 98 on his last test, the total sum of his test scores will be \\( 91n + 98 \\). The new arithmetic mean after the last test is 92, so the total sum of his test scores after also last test is \\( 92(n + 1) \\).\n\nSetting up the equation:\n\\[ 91n + 98 = 92(n + 1) \\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying:\n\\[ 91n + 98 = 92n + 92 \\]\n\\[ 98 - 92 = 92n - 91n \\]\n\\[ 6 = n \\]\n\nSo, Brian has taken 6 tests before his last test. Including the last test, he takes a total of 7 tests.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is 7. I hope it is correct"
"Let's denote the total number of games Kent played on Saturday as \\( T \\).\n\nGiven that Kent won 25% of the games, the number of games he won is \\( 0.25T \\).\n\nWe also know that he lost 12 games. Therefore, the number of games he won plus the number of games he lost equals equal the total number of games he played:\n\n\\[ 0.25T + 12 = T \\]\n\nTo find \\( T \\), we rearrange the equation:\n\n\\[ T - 0.25T = 12 \\]\n\\[ 0.75T = 12 \\]\n\\[ T = \\frac{12}{0.75} \\]\n\\[ T = 16 \\]\n\nSo, Kent played a total of 16 games on Saturday.\n\nSince he won 25% of the games, the number of games he won is:\n\n\\[ 0.25 \\times 16 = 4 \\]\n\nTherefore, Kent won \\(\\boxed{4}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4}\\)."
"The area of a trapezoid is given by $\\frac{1}{2} \\times (\\text{base}_1 + \\text{base}_2) \\times \\text{height}$. Let the height of the trapezoid be $h$. Then the area of trapezoid $ABCD$ is:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{2} \\times (AB + CD) \\times h = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (20 + 12) \\times h = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 32 \\times h = 16h.$$\n\nThe area of triangle $ACB$ is:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{2} \\times AB \\times h = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 20 \\times h = 10h.$$\n\nThe ratio of the area of triangle $ACB$ to the area of trapezoid $ABCD$ is:\n\n$$\\frac{10h}{16h} = \\frac{10}{16} = \\boxed{\\frac{5}{16}}.$$\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $\\frac{5}{16}$. I hope it is correct"
