"To solve the inequality \\(-2 < 4 + 3x < 2\\), we will break it down into two separate inequalities and solve each one.\n\nFirst, solve the inequality \\(-2 < 4 + 3x\\):\n\n1. Subtract 4 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   -6 < 3x\n   \\]\n2. Divide both sides by 3:\n   \\[\n   -2 < x\n   \\]\n\nNext, solve the inequality \\(4 + 3x < 2\\):\n\n1. Subtract 4 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   3x < -2\n   \\]\n2. Divide both sides by 3:\n   \\[\n   x < -\\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n\nCombining these two results, we get:\n\\[\n-2 < x < -\\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe integers that satisfy this inequality are \\(-1\\) and \\(-2\\). Therefore, the number of integers \\(x\\) satisfying that satisfy \\(-2 < 4 + 3x < 2\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."
